__notes__.note.mass.setting-setting-imports:
Notation follows the geometry and action papers \cite{ButlerT6, ButlerT10}: spacetime signature $(+,-,-,-)$, dimension $n = 4$ except where $n$ is displayed, ambient (tractor) bundle of rank six with fibre metric of signature $(2,4)$, and units $G = c = 1$; $\hbar$ is kept as a formal parameter where quantisation is discussed and set to $1$ elsewhere. In a scale $g$ the tractor splits as $V = (\sigma, \mu_{a}, \rho)$ with
\begin{equation}
\ip{(\sigma,\mu,\rho)}{(\sigma',\mu',\rho')} = \sigma\rho' + \rho\sigma' + g(\mu,\mu'),
\end{equation}
the hyperbolic pair on the outer slots and the spacetime metric on the middle. The Schouten tensor is $P_{ab}$, its trace $J = g^{ab}P_{ab}$, and $\Box = g^{ab}\nabla_{a}\nabla_{b}$ is the wave operator, so that the Klein-Gordon equation for mass $m$ reads $(\Box + m^{2})\sigma = 0$. Throughout, $H^{2} := \Lambda/3$, so that $Q(I) = H^{2}$ for the frozen point below; $H$ is the Hubble rate of the de Sitter background, and ``Hubble units'' means $H = 1$.

__notes__.note.singularity.setting-gauge-theory-ex:
In GTG, gravity is a pair of gauge fields on flat spacetime: the position-dependent linear map $\hb(a)$ on vectors, gauging local frame rescalings, and the connection bivector $\Omega(a)$, gauging local Lorentz rotations \cite{LDG1998} (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}). The covariant derivative and the field strength are
\begin{equation}
D_a M \;=\; a\cdot\nabla M + \Omega(a) \times M, \qquad
R(a \wedge b) \;=\; a\cdot\nabla\,\Omega(b) - b\cdot\nabla\,\Omega(a) + \Omega(a) \times \Omega(b),
\end{equation}
with $A \times B := \tfrac12(AB - BA)$ the commutator product, so that $[D_a, D_b]M = R(a \wedge b) \times M$. The curvature operator on physical bivectors is $\mathcal{R}(B) = -R(\hb(B))$, in the store's curvature sign: Lasenby, Doran and Gull \cite{LDG1998} and \cite[Ch.~14]{DoranLasenby2003} use $R(\hb(B))$, and this single overall sign is the only difference. Contracting once and twice with the vector derivative gives the Ricci and scalar curvatures, $\mathcal{R}(b) = \partial_a \cdot \mathcal{R}(a \wedge b)$ and $\mathcal{R} = \partial_a \cdot \mathcal{R}(a)$, and the field equations of the theory are
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}(a) - \tfrac12\, a\, \mathcal{R} \;=\; -8\pi\, T(a), \qquad
D \wedge \hb^{T}(a) \;=\; \hb^{T}(\nabla \wedge a) ,
\end{equation}
where $\hb^{T}$ is the adjoint, $a \cdot \hb^{T}(b) = \hb(a) \cdot b$. The first is Einstein's equation in bivector-contracted vector form, with $T(a)$ the matter stress-energy (in the sign of \cite{LDG1998} it reads $+8\pi T(a)$); the second is the vanishing of torsion, which determines $\Omega$ algebraically from $\hb$ and its derivatives, so that $\hb$ carries the theory's entire dynamical content. The metric every observer measures is
\begin{equation}
g(a,b) \;=\; \hb^{-1}(a) \cdot \hb^{-1}(b),
\end{equation}
and the inverse in that formula is where the theory can fail: the geometric reading of the theory exists exactly where $\hb$ is invertible. The theory's two gauge freedoms act by
\begin{equation}
\hb(a) \;\mapsto\; \hb(\tilde U a U), \quad\text{equivalently}\quad \hb^{T}(a) \;\mapsto\; U\,\hb^{T}(a)\,\tilde U, \qquad
\Omega(a) \;\mapsto\; U\,\Omega(a)\,\tilde U - 2\,(a\cdot\nabla U)\,\tilde U
\end{equation}
for a rotor field $U$ (rotation gauge), together with the position-gauge displacements, which act by pullback \cite{LDG1998}; the curvature transforms covariantly under both, $\mathcal{R}(B) \mapsto U\,\mathcal{R}(\tilde U B U)\,\tilde U$ under the rotation gauge, so every scalar built from it is gauge-invariant. Every operation in these displays is interior to $\Cl(1,3)$: nothing invokes a curved manifold beyond the flat background.

interior.non-null-stabilisers-section-th.formal:
Let $I \in V$ with $Q(I) \neq 0$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the stabiliser subalgebra of $I$ is $\Lambda^2(I^\perp) \cong \mathfrak{so}(I^\perp)$, reductive, of dimension $10$, with trivial nilradical: $\mathfrak{so}(1,4)$ if $Q(I) > 0$ and $\mathfrak{so}(2,3)$ if $Q(I) < 0$;
\item the hyperplane section $M_I := \{X \in V : Q(X) = 0,\ \ip{X}{I} = 1\}$ is, under $Y := X - I/Q(I)$, the pseudosphere $\{Y \in I^\perp : Q(Y) = -1/Q(I)\}$, a complete constant-curvature Lorentzian $4$-manifold on which the stabiliser acts as the isometry group: de Sitter space for $Q(I) > 0$, anti-de Sitter space for $Q(I) < 0$;
\item the polar hypersurface $\{\ip{X}{I} = 0\}$ meets the quadric in the projectivised null cone of $I^\perp$: for $Q(I) > 0$ a spacelike conformal infinity ($S^3$ with its standard conformal structure), for $Q(I) < 0$ a timelike one.
\end{enumerate}

interior.stabiliser-rotates-parabolic-re.formal:
The stabiliser of the frozen point $I_g$ in the structure group of the completion is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item for $\Lambda = 0$: the stabiliser of a null vector, the Poincar\'e group $SO(1,3) \ltimes \R^4$ (Proposition \claimref{interior.stabilisers-flat-model}), a group with $4$-dimensional nilradical: the parabolic situation, whose transvections are the obstruction structure of \cite{ButlerT1};
\item for $\Lambda > 0$: the de Sitter group $SO(1,4)$, reductive, acting on the Einstein section as its isometry group, with conformal infinity the spacelike polar $3$-sphere $\ip{X}{I} = 0$;
\item for $\Lambda < 0$: the anti-de Sitter group $SO(2,3)$, reductive, with timelike polar infinity.
\end{enumerate}
In short: $\Lambda$ measures precisely the failure of infinity to be parabolic.

synthesis.c-loc-entropy-clause-curvature.formal:
Signature $(-,+,+,+)$ in this statement and its proof, with $Q(v)$ the square of a vector $v$; the store's $(+,-,-,-)$ changes the sign of $Q$ and of every inner product below and nothing else. Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}). Let the flat tangent data of a local Rindler horizon element at a point $p$ of its invertible region be the space of physical vectors at $p$ with the flat product (the tangent Minkowski space $(T_{p}M, g_{p})$ of the derived metric), a future null ray $[\ell]$ and the spacelike two-plane $\Pi \subset \ell^{\perp}$ of the edge element, as in Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-jacobson-clausius-1995}. Call (Loc) the condition: \emph{the entropy of a small patch of a horizon is, at leading order in the size of the patch, the integral over its cross-section of a density $s$ per unit area whose value at each point depends only on the isometry class of the flat tangent data there; in particular $s$ is independent of the curvature (the field strength $\mathcal{R}$) and of the stress-energy $T$ at the point, and of the normalisation of the boost.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(One constant.)} The proper orthochronous Lorentz group acts transitively on pairs (future null ray, spacelike two-plane orthogonal to it): for future null $\ell$, $n$ with $\ell\cdot n \neq 0$, at most two reflections in spacelike vectors carry $(\ell_{0}, n_{0}, \Pi_{0}) = (e_{0} + e_{1}, e_{0} - e_{1}, \langle e_{2}, e_{3}\rangle)$ to $(c\ell, \mu n, \{\ell, n\}^{\perp})$ with $c, \mu > 0$ ($c = 1$ unless $\ell$ is a positive multiple of $\ell_{0}$), carrying an orthonormal basis of $\Pi_{0}$ to one of $\{\ell, n\}^{\perp}$. The stabiliser of the data contains the boosts of the $(\ell, n)$ plane, which rescale $\ell$ by every positive factor (the normalisation of the boost flow, through which the acceleration $a$ labels it) and fix $\Pi$ pointwise, and the rotations of $\Pi$; both preserve the area element of $\Pi$. All tangent spaces are isometric: in Gauge Theory Gravity the physical vectors at every point are the vectors of one Clifford algebra with its fixed flat product. Hence under (Loc) the density is one constant $\eta$, independent of the point, the null direction, the edge plane, the frame and the acceleration.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(The number, with no count.)} Assume (P) and (R) and Hawking's thermodynamic hypothesis as in Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quarter-from-temperature} (internal energy $M$, $dS = dM/T$ along the Schwarzschild family, $S \to 0$ as $M \to 0$), so that $S(M) = A/4$; and assume that this thermodynamic entropy is the integral of the density of (Loc) over the horizon cross-section up to $o(A)$ as $M \to \infty$ (the hole's entropy and the horizon density describe one set of degrees of freedom, not two). Then $\eta = \lim_{M\to\infty}S/A = \tfrac14$. The value depends on the temperature normalisation and on Hawking's hypothesis: with a period $p$ of imaginary Killing time in place of the regular $2\pi/\kappa$, the same steps give $\eta = p\kappa/8\pi$. The entropies the store holds for the de Sitter horizon, $S_{\Lambda} = \pi N^{2}$ for area $4\pi N^{2}$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.de-sitter-entropy-pi-n-squared}), and for the Kerr horizon, $A_{+}/4$ (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-kerr-surface-gravity-entropy}), give the same $\eta$ under the same identification. No count and no $\nu$ enter. The count form $\eta = 4\pi\nu\langle s\rangle$ of clause (i) of version 1 of Premise \claimref{synthesis.c-loc-local-horizon-premise} then holds if and only if $16\pi\nu\langle s\rangle = 1$, the area-law condition of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.clausius-route-newton-constant-is-residue}(iii).
\item[(iii)] \emph{(What clause (i) adds.)} Under the hypotheses of (ii) and at leading order, the entropy content of clause (i) of version 1 of Premise \claimref{synthesis.c-loc-local-horizon-premise} (the clause (Loc) of the current version), an entropy $\eta\,\delta A$ with $\eta$ one universal constant (hypothesis (b) of Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-jacobson-clausius-1995}), is equivalent to (Loc), with $\eta = \tfrac14$. Its mode-count content, boundary modes at the epoch density, is not implied by (Loc).
\item[(iv)] \emph{(Constancy from Clausius alone.)} Independently of (Loc): if the entropy of a local Rindler horizon patch is $\int\eta\,dA$ for a $C^{1}$ function $\eta$ on the invertible region, the Clausius relation of Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-jacobson-clausius-1995}(c) at first order in the patch forces $k \cdot D\eta = 0$ ($k^{a}\nabla_{a}\eta = 0$) for every physical null $k$ at every point, hence $\eta$ constant on a connected region: the heat $\delta Q$ and the area change are of second order in the affine extent $\lambda_{0}$ of the patch (vanishing expansion at $p$), while a gradient of $\eta$ along the generators changes the entropy at first order, by $-\lambda_{0}\,k^{a}\nabla_{a}\eta$ per unit area.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.clausius-heat-exceeds-created-.formal:
Let the boundary modes of a local horizon patch be organised in windows $F$, finite sets of fermionic modes with Killing weights $\omega_j \ge 0$ and boost dynamics $H_F = \sum_{j \in F}\omega_j N_j$, as in Definition \claimref{obj.hawking.boundary-mode-algebra-windows-boost-dynamics} (whose weights are positive; the value $\omega = 0$ is admitted here as the limit at which the registered residue sits when each counted unit is one mode; under the weight clause of version 3 of Premise (Q) the registered value is also realised by two arrays per unit at nonzero weight), with inverse temperature $\beta = 1/T$ in Killing time and $x_j = \beta\omega_j$, and write $n(x) = 1/(e^{x} + 1)$ and $s(x) = \ln(1 + e^{-x}) + x\,n(x)$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.boundary-mode-thermal-entropy}). Consider the hypotheses:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(R$_{\mathrm{inst}}$)] at each instant considered, the state of the patch's boundary modes restricts to each window as a KMS state of its boost dynamics at $\beta$, the weights being fixed (the temperature clause (R$_{\mathrm{loc}}$) of Premise \claimref{synthesis.c-loc-local-horizon-premise} read at every instant);
\item[(Cl)] between two instants, the boundary-mode entropy changes by $\delta S = \delta Q/T$, where $\delta Q$ is the boost energy matter delivers (the Clausius hypothesis (H1) of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.einstein-equations-from-c-loc-and-clausius}, with the entropy carried by the boundary modes as in clause (Loc));
\item[(E)] the boost energy matter delivers equals the change $\delta E$ of the boundary modes' Killing energy $\langle H\rangle$.
\end{enumerate}
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(No heat into a fixed window.)} Under (R$_{\mathrm{inst}}$) a window's state is $\varrho_{I} = e^{-\beta H_F}/Z_F$ (Theorem \claimref{singularity.kms-uniqueness-factor}), so between two instants at which (R$_{\mathrm{inst}}$) holds with the same window, its entropy and Killing energy are unchanged.
\item \emph{(Created modes.)} If between two instants obeying (R$_{\mathrm{inst}}$) the mode set grows by a set $C$ and loses a set $D$ (other modes unchanged), then $\delta S = \sum_{C}s(x_j) - \sum_{D}s(x_j)$ and $\delta E = T\bigl(\sum_{C}x_j n(x_j) - \sum_{D}x_j n(x_j)\bigr)$.
\item \emph{(The deficit.)} Under (R$_{\mathrm{inst}}$) and (Cl),
\[
\delta Q - \delta E \;=\; T\Bigl(\sum_{C}\ln\bigl(1 + e^{-x_j}\bigr) - \sum_{D}\ln\bigl(1 + e^{-x_j}\bigr)\Bigr).
\]
If all created and lost modes share one weight $x \ge 0$ (the common-weight setting of the registered residue), with net number $\delta m$, then $\delta Q = T s(x)\,\delta m$, $\delta E = T x\,n(x)\,\delta m$ and $\delta Q - \delta E = T\ln(1 + e^{-x})\,\delta m$, which is nonzero whenever $\delta Q \neq 0$; the fraction of the heat that is Killing energy is $x\,n(x)/s(x) < 1$, and it is $0$ at $x = 0$, where $\delta Q = T\ln 2\,\delta m$. The same holds if modes are only created ($D = \emptyset$), for any weights. Hence (R$_{\mathrm{inst}}$), (Cl) and (E) are inconsistent whenever heat crosses in either of these settings.
\item \emph{(The other reading.)} If (R$_{\mathrm{inst}}$) is required only at the earlier instant, a fixed window can receive Killing energy $\delta E$ with (E), and its first law then gives Clausius at first order (the route of the result filed alongside, synthesis.clausius-is-window-first-law-plus-boost-charge-conservation); by (1) the new state is not KMS for the window's boost at $\beta$ unless $\delta E = 0$. An exact example: a resonant number-conserving exchange between a window mode at $e^{-x} = 1/3$ (occupation $1/4$) and an occupied matter mode, rotating the one-particle sector by $\cos\theta = 3/5$, conserves total occupation and leaves the window mode at occupation $73/100$, not the KMS value.
\end{enumerate}
So on the store's premises (H1) needs one further structure: an energy carrier that is neither matter nor the boundary modes' Killing energy, taking $T\ln(1 + e^{-x})$ per created mode (all of the heat at zero weight), with a rule by which matter's boost energy creates modes; or a dynamics of the weights, which the boundary-mode Definition does not provide; or a weakening of (R$_{\mathrm{loc}}$) to the state before the crossing.

synthesis.frozen-point-integrand-gives-e.formal:
Work in $\Cl(2,4)$ with $\nO$, $\nInf$ as in Theorem \claimref{synthesis.gtg-tractor-gauge-field-reproduces-tractor-connection} ($\nO \cdot \nInf = 1$), canonical tractor $X = \nO$, frozen point $I = \nInf + \tfrac{\Lambda}{6}\nO$ and $V = X - \tfrac{3}{\Lambda}I$ (Theorem \claimref{dark-energy.frozen-point-makes-stelle-west-vector}), and $\epsilon$ with the orientation of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.frozen-point-integrand-on-tractor-transport-is-weyl-squared}. A $\Cl(2,4)$ gauge field on flat spacetime whose $\nInf$ block is nondegenerate is, in the splitting of that theorem,
\[
A(a) \;=\; \Omega(a) \;-\; \nInf \wedge \hb^{-1}(a) \;+\; \nO \wedge S\bigl(\hb^{-1}(a)\bigr) \;+\; \beta(a)\,\nInf \wedge \nO ,
\]
with $\Omega$ the rotation gauge field, $\hb^{-1}(a)$ the coframe, $S$ a field of linear maps of spacetime vectors (the special-conformal slot; the normal tractor connection has $S = \Sch$) and $\beta$ a scalar one-form (the dilation). Split $S$, with respect to the derived metric, into its trace part $\sigma\,\id$, its antisymmetric part and its trace-free symmetric part. A \emph{slot field space} varies $\hb$ and $\Omega$ and, for each of the three parts, either freezes it at its Lorentz-invariant value ($\sigma = \rho$, a constant; the other two zero) or varies it as an independent field; $\beta$ is zero or varied. Fields are torsion-free (the curvature of $\Omega$ obeys the first Bianchi identity). $E_{4}$, $|W|^{2}$, $|\Ric_{0}|^{2}$ and $R^{2}$ denote the Euler density, the Weyl square, the square of the trace-free Ricci map and of the Ricci scalar (index readings, store's curvature sign), $\mathcal{L}$ the density $\epsilon(I, V, F, F) = \epsilon(I, X, F, F)$ as a multiple of the volume form.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(What the integrand sees; proved.)} $\mathcal{L}$ depends on $A$ only through the Lorentz block of $F$, $F_{W}(B) = -\mathcal{R}(B) + T_{S}(B)$ with $T_{S}(a \wedge b) = a \wedge S(b) + S(a) \wedge b$ (on frame vectors), and on the frozen point only through the blade $I \wedge X = \nInf \wedge \nO$, so not on $\Lambda$; it does not depend on $\beta$. The connection preserves $\nInf + \rho\,\nO$ if and only if $S = \rho\,\id$ and $\beta = 0$.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(The free slot is the normal tractor connection; proved.)} $\mathcal{L}$ is quadratic in $S$ with a nondegenerate quadratic part on all sixteen components. With the whole slot varied, its equation has the unique solution $S = \Sch$, the Schouten map, at which $F_{W} = -\mathcal{W}$ and $\mathcal{L} = \tfrac14 |W|^{2}$. So on the torsion-free conformal Cartan connections (values in all bivectors, nondegenerate soldering, curvature of $\Omega$ obeying the first Bianchi identity, gauged by the parabolic group of the line of $X$), with the whole slot varied, the on-shell connection is the normal tractor connection, and the reduced functional is the Weyl-squared functional of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.frozen-point-integrand-on-tractor-transport-is-weyl-squared}, with the Bach equations (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-conformal-gravity-bach-equations-and-conformal-gauge-form}(a)). The equation for $\Omega$ on this field space with torsion is not treated here. This proves in the series the elimination step of Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-conformal-gravity-bach-equations-and-conformal-gauge-form}(b).
\item[(iii)] \emph{(The lattice; proved.)} After eliminating the varied parts, with $\Lambda = 6\rho$:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item symmetric part frozen (antisymmetric part frozen or varied, $\beta$ zero or varied): $\tfrac14 E_{4} - 2\rho\,(R - 2\Lambda)$, Euler, Einstein-Hilbert and volume terms with the frozen point's $\Lambda$;
  \item trace varied, trace-free part frozen: $\tfrac14 E_{4} - \tfrac{1}{24}R^{2}$, with no Einstein-Hilbert or volume term;
  \item trace frozen, trace-free part varied: $\tfrac14 E_{4} + \tfrac12 |\Ric_{0}|^{2} - 2\rho\,(R - 2\Lambda)$;
  \item both varied: $\tfrac14 E_{4} + \tfrac12 |\Ric_{0}|^{2} - \tfrac{1}{24}R^{2} = \tfrac14 |W|^{2}$, as in (ii).
  \end{itemize}
  At $\rho = 0$ (the connection preserves the null tractor $\nInf$) the first row is $\tfrac14 E_{4}$ alone. On torsion-free fields the antisymmetric part's equation sets it to zero, so it is inert; with torsion it is not.
\item[(iv)] \emph{(Einstein exactly on the frozen rows; proved, given the imported Facts.)} The reduced functional has Euler-Lagrange equations of second order, which are then Einstein's equations with $\Lambda = 6\rho$, if and only if the symmetric part of the slot is frozen at $\rho\,\id$ with $\rho \neq 0$: in the other rows the remainder over $\tfrac14 E_{4}$ and the Einstein-Hilbert part, namely $-\tfrac{1}{24}R^{2}$, $\tfrac12|\Ric_{0}|^{2}$ or their sum, is not variationally trivial. So the integrand gives Einstein's equations if and only if the field space, with the inert directions set to zero, is the one preserving $I$; the field space need not preserve any tractor off shell (rows with $\beta$ or the antisymmetric part varied preserve none for generic values of those fields).
\item[(v)] \emph{(The no-inverse-frame hypothesis does not separate the rows; proved.)} In every row $\mathcal{L}$ is a polynomial in $\Omega$, the coframe and the slot one-form $S(\hb^{-1}(a))$, with no inverse frame, and invariant under the Lorentz and dilation gauge transformations that fix the blade. In the all-varied row the inverse frame enters only on shell, through $S = \Sch$. So the hypothesis (P) of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.curvature-only-tractor-actions-are-macdowell-mansouri} excludes the normal tractor connection as an off-shell function of $(\hb, \Omega)$, and the forcing of $S = \rho\,\id$ (its clause (vi), proved as clause (i) of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.inverse-frame-free-tractor-gauge-field-preserves-a-frozen-point}) holds only for slots that are functions of $(\hb, \Omega)$, the premise (T) there; the $I$-preserving field space rests on (T), that the symmetric part of the slot is not a field, which (P) does not supply. In invariant form (T) with (P) reads: the varied connection admits a parallel tractor $I$ with $\ip{X}{I} = 1$ (Premise \claimref{synthesis.field-space-premise-parallel-frozen-point}, a holonomy reduction to the stabiliser of $I$ in the sense of Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-holonomy-reductions-of-cartan-geometries}(a)), and $S = \rho\,\id$, $\beta = 0$ is that statement in the gauge where $I$ is constant.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-conformal-gravity-bac.formal:
Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on four-dimensional flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}) with derived metric $g$ and Weyl map $\mathcal{W}$ of its field strength (index reading $W_{abcd}$, round-sphere-positive convention).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Bach.) The functional $\int W_{abcd}W^{abcd}\,\mathrm{vol}$ is invariant under constant and local rescalings of the metric in four dimensions, and its Euler-Lagrange expression under variation of $g$ is a constant multiple of the Bach tensor, in index reading $B_{ab} = \nabla^{c}\nabla^{d}W_{acbd} + \tfrac12 R^{cd}W_{acbd}$, symmetric, trace-free and of fourth order in $g$. $B$ vanishes on every Einstein field ($\Ric$ a multiple of $g$, any $\Lambda$), and, since it is conformally covariant, on every field conformal to an Einstein field; the converse fails.
\item[(b)] (Kaku, Townsend and van Nieuwenhuizen.) Four-dimensional conformal gravity is the gauge theory of the conformal group (in the series, the bivectors of $\Cl(2,4)$): for a conformal gauge field with the conventional constraints (zero torsion, and the special-conformal part solved algebraically in terms of the curvature, the Schouten tensor), the functional formed as the $\epsilon$-contracted square of the Lorentz block of the conformal field strength is, up to a constant factor and a topological term, the Weyl-squared functional of (a).
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{Bach1921} for (a); \cite{KakuTownsendVanNieuwenhuizen1977} for (b).

synthesis.imported-jacobson-clausius-199.formal:
Units $\hbar = c = 1$. Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on flat spacetime with derived metric $g$ (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}). Suppose that through every point $p$ of its invertible region and for every future physically null direction $k$ at $p$ ($Q(\hb^{-1}(k)) = 0$; components $k^{a}$), the local Rindler horizon (the past horizon of a small spacelike two-surface through $p$ whose null normals $k$ have vanishing expansion and shear at $p$; in a normal gauge at $p$, $\hb(p) = 1$ and $\Omega(p) = 0$, it is at leading order a piece of the null hyperplane through $p$ orthogonal to $k$) satisfies:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] its temperature is the Unruh temperature $T = a/2\pi$ of the boost flow $\chi^{a}$ of acceleration $a$;
\item[(b)] its entropy is $\eta$ per unit area, $\delta S = \eta\,\delta A$, with $\eta$ one universal constant;
\item[(c)] the Clausius relation $\delta Q = T\,\delta S$ holds, with $\delta Q = \int T_{ab}\chi^{a}\,d\Sigma^{b}$ the boost energy of matter crossing it, and $\delta A$ given by the focusing of the generators through the Raychaudhuri equation.
\end{enumerate}
Then $T_{ab}k^{a}k^{b} = (\eta/2\pi)R_{ab}k^{a}k^{b}$ for every null $k^{a}$, with $R_{ab}$ the Ricci tensor of the round-sphere-positive convention, that is $k \cdot T(k) = -(\eta/2\pi)\,k \cdot \mathcal{R}(k)$ for every physical null $k$ in the store's sign of the curvature operator; and with the conservation of $T$ ($\nabla^{a}T_{ab} = 0$) and the contracted Bianchi identity, in the signature $(-,+,+,+)$ of the source,
\[
R_{ab} - \tfrac12 R\,g_{ab} + \Lambda g_{ab} \;=\; \frac{2\pi}{\eta}\,T_{ab},
\]
with $\Lambda$ an undetermined constant: Einstein's equation with $G = 1/(4\eta)$, which for the gravitational field reads $\mathcal{G}(a) + \Lambda a = -(2\pi/\eta)\,T(a)$ in the store's sign. \emph{Provenance:} \cite{Jacobson1995}.

synthesis.imported-lovelock-and-anderson.formal:
Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on four-dimensional flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}) with derived metric $g$, and call an expression \emph{natural} if it is built from $g$ and its derivatives up to a fixed order covariantly, so that it transforms as a tensor under displacement and rotation gauge transformations (in a curved-spacetime reading: a concomitant of the metric, equivariant under diffeomorphisms).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Lovelock.) A natural symmetric map $E(a)$ built from $g$ and its derivatives up to second order that is divergence-free for every field is $E(a) = c_{1}\,\mathcal{G}(a) + c_{2}\,a$ with constants $c_{1}$, $c_{2}$, $\mathcal{G}$ the Einstein map in the store's sign. In particular, if a natural Lagrangian density of $g$ has an Euler-Lagrange expression of second order, that expression is of this form; its vacuum solutions with $c_{1} \neq 0$ have the one value $\Lambda = c_{2}/c_{1}$ of the cosmological term. In $n$ dimensions the divergence-free second-order expressions are the Lovelock family, which for $n = 4$ reduces to these two terms.
\item[(b)] (Variationally trivial natural Lagrangians.) A natural Lagrangian density of a four-dimensional metric whose Euler-Lagrange expression vanishes identically is, locally, $c_{E}E_{4} + c_{P}P_{4}$ plus the divergence of a natural current, with $E_{4}$ the Euler density ($R_{abcd}R^{abcd} - 4R_{ab}R^{ab} + R^{2}$ in index reading) and $P_{4}$ the Pontryagin density ($\tfrac12\epsilon^{cdef}R_{abcd}R^{ab}{}_{ef}$ in index reading), with constants $c_{E}$, $c_{P}$. Hence two natural Lagrangians of $g$ with the same Euler-Lagrange expressions differ by such a combination.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{Lovelock1971, Lovelock1972} for (a); \cite{AndersonDuchamp1980, Anderson1984} for (b) and for the inverse problem of the calculus of variations for natural operators.

